.TH CLIPMUNGE 5 "2026-09-03" "clipmunge" "File Formats"
.SH NAME
clipmunge \- configuration file for clipmunge(1)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/clipmunge/config.lua
.PP
.RI ( ~/.config/clipmunge/config.lua
when
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
is unset.)
.SH DESCRIPTION
The file is Lua. Loading it is what defines the rules: the config calls
.B clipmunge.rule
once per rule, in the order the rules should be tried.
.PP
Lua rather than a declarative format because the rules that are worth having
are not substitutions. Canonicalising a shopping link means picking an
identifier out of a path; deciding which of several trackers a bare identifier
belongs to means a table lookup. As regular expressions with templates those
are write\-only; as five lines of code they are obvious. The one rule common
enough to be everybody's, dropping tracking parameters, is in the library
already \- see
.BR clipmunge.url.strip_params .
.PP
A missing file is an error, not an empty rule set.
.PP
The file is trusted: it can set
.B notify_command
and thereby run a program. Its
.I rules
are not trusted in the same way \- the interpreter never loads
.BR io ,
.B os
or the C loader, so a handler cannot touch the filesystem, the network or
another process, and it runs on an instruction and memory budget. See the
SECURITY section of
.BR clipmunge (1).
.SH RULES
.B clipmunge.rule
takes one table:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
clipmunge.rule {
  name = "tickets",
  match = [[^([A-Z]+)-(\\d+)$]],
  when = "plain-only",
  handler = function(kind, number)
    return clipmunge.link("https://tracker/" .. kind .. number)
  end,
}
.EE
.RE
.TP
.B name
Used in log messages. Optional but worth setting; without it a failing rule
reports itself as \fB?\fR.
.TP
.B match
A regular expression, applied to the selection's plain text. Required.
.IP
This is a real regular expression \- the Rust
.B regex
crate \- and not a Lua pattern. Alternation and bounded repetition such as
.B \\ed{6,9}
work. Lookaround and backreferences do not exist, which is the price of
matching in time linear in the input; the same property means a pattern from
somebody else's rule set cannot hang the daemon.
.IP
Patterns are compiled when the config loads, so mistakes surface at start\-up
and on reload rather than on a later copy.
.IP
Beware that Lua's own
.B string.match
is still available inside handlers and takes Lua patterns, which look similar
and are not the same language.
.TP
.B when
.B "always"
(the default) or
.BR "plain-only" .
With
.BR plain-only ,
the rule is skipped when the selection already carries a rich flavour such as
.BR text/html .
A browser copying a link already knows the correct href, and a guess should
not overwrite it. Rules that clean URLs generally want the default, because
cruft arrives from the browser in the first place.
.TP
.B handler
A function receiving the capture groups of
.B match
as its arguments, group 1 first. A group that did not participate arrives as
.BR nil .
Required.
.SH HANDLER RESULTS
A handler returns one of:
.TP
.B nil
Decline. The next rule is tried; if none matches, the clipboard is untouched.
.TP
a string
Replace the plain text with it. Other flavours are dropped.
.TP
a table
Each key is a MIME type and each value is the bytes for it, with two
exceptions.
.B text
sets the whole text/plain family at once, and
.B notify
is a message for the user rather than for the clipboard.
.PP
Only what the table lists is published. Nothing is inherited from the incoming
selection, deliberately: cleaning a URL in
.B text/plain
while passing an untouched
.B text/html
through would put the original tracking link back the moment somebody pasted
into a rich editor.
.PP
The first rule to return something other than
.B nil
wins. Rules do not chain; two rules that fed each other would loop, and the
loop guard cannot see inside a single pass.
.SH LIBRARY
.TP
.BI clipmunge.link( url [, text ])
Builds the common shape: plain text, an
.B <a href>
for
.BR text/html ,
and
.BR chromium/x-source-url .
\fItext\fR defaults to \fIurl\fR. Escaping is handled here.
.TP
.BI clipmunge.html_escape( s )
For tables assembled by hand.
.TP
.BI clipmunge.url.strip_params( url "[, " patterns ])
Removes tracking parameters from \fIurl\fR. Returns the cleaned URL and a
list of the keys that were dropped, or
.B nil
when nothing matched.
.IP
That
.B nil
matters as much as the cleaning does. A rule that rewrites URLs is handed its
own output on the next round, so answering \(lqno change\(rq for an already
clean URL is what keeps it from looping.
.IP
Keys are matched case\-insensitively and a trailing
.B *
matches a prefix, so
.B utm_*
covers the whole family. Only parameter names are considered; a tracker name
appearing in a
.I value
is left alone. The fragment survives, and the
.B ?
disappears entirely when nothing is left before it.
.IP
\fIpatterns\fR replaces the built\-in list rather than adding to it. To
extend the default, start from
.BR clipmunge.url.default_junk .
.TP
.B clipmunge.url.default_junk
The built\-in list, as a table: the
.B utm_*
family,
.BR fbclid ,
.BR gclid ,
.BR msclkid ,
.BR igshid ,
.BR si ,
.BR yclid ,
.B spm
and their neighbours.
.PP
Escaping belongs to whoever builds the markup. Once a handler has returned
bytes, the daemon cannot tell data from tags, so a table you build yourself is
yours to escape \- otherwise clipboard content ends up injected into the
markup somebody pastes.
.SH SETTINGS
.B clipmunge.settings
takes a table of globals. An unrecognised key is a warning at load time, not a
silent no\-op: a config that reads as if it sets something has to either set
it or say why not.
.TP
.B notify_command
The argument list used to send notifications, default
.BR "{ \(dqnotify-send\(dq, \(dq-a\(dq, \(dqclipmunge\(dq, \(dqclipmunge\(dq, \(dq{}\(dq }" .
The element
.B {}
is replaced, whole, by the rule's
.B notify
text.
.IP
It is a list, not a command line, and no shell is involved. Clipboard content
is the least trustworthy input there is, and substituting it into a shell
string would be a command injection with the internet as its source.
.IP
This setting is the one place a config reaches outside the process, and it is
deliberate. It is also the reason to read a rule set before installing it: a
handler cannot start a process, but the file it came in can name one here.
.IP
Nothing is sent unless a rule asks for it, so a config with no
.B notify
in any handler is silent regardless of this setting.
.TP
.B secret_mimes
Advertised flavours whose presence makes clipmunge leave a selection alone
entirely \- not rewritten, not read, and therefore not logged even under
.BR \-\-debug .
Default
.BR "{ \(dqx-kde-passwordManagerHint\(dq }" .
.IP
The list replaces the built\-in one rather than extending it, the same way
.B clipmunge.url.strip_params
takes a replacement list. An empty table is allowed and means \(lqhonour no
such hint\(rq; that is a position somebody may hold, and this is where it is
visible.
.IP
Only the flavour's presence is checked, never its value. Fetching a six\-byte
hint would cost a pipe round trip with a source that may never answer, and a
source that advertised the hint has already said what it means.
.IP
.B This is partial and depends entirely on the other application.
The hint is opt\-in for whoever owns the selection, and most owners do not opt
in. Measured on Firefox 154: copying from
.B about:logins
sets it; copying out of an
.B <input type=password>
does not, and neither does the 1Password browser extension. What actually
keeps a password out of a rewrite is that no rule matches it \- see LOOPS and
the note on catch\-all patterns.
.SH EXAMPLE
.RS 4
.EX
clipmunge.settings {
  notify_command = { "notify-send", "-a", "clipmunge", "clipmunge", "{}" },
}

clipmunge.rule {
  name = "detrack",
  match = [[^(https?://\\S+)$]],
  handler = function(url)
    local clean, dropped = clipmunge.url.strip_params(url)
    -- nil means nothing matched, which is also what stops this rule from
    -- rewriting its own output for ever.
    if not clean then return nil end
    local out = clipmunge.link(clean, clean)
    out.notify = "Dropped " .. table.concat(dropped, ", ")
    return out
  end,
}
.EE
.RE
.SH CATCH-ALL PATTERNS
A pattern such as
.B ^(.*)$
matches everything you ever copy, passwords included, and hands it to a
handler. That is a supported thing to write and occasionally the right one,
but it is the only way an ordinary rule set can end up rewriting a secret: the
shipped rules match a URL or a bare identifier and a password is neither.
.PP
.B secret_mimes
above helps when the source marks its content, which is uncommon. It is a
second line, not the first one.
.SH LOOPS
Every selection clipmunge publishes carries a private
.B application/x-clipmunge
flavour, and a selection carrying it is ignored. That handles the daemon
seeing its own work.
.PP
It does not handle a rule whose output matches its own pattern, because that
happens within one pass. A rule that rewrites URLs has to be idempotent, and
the usual way is to return
.B nil
when there is nothing left to change, as the example does.
.SH SPLITTING THE CONFIG
.B require
works and searches the config directory, so a large rule set can live in
several files next to
.IR config.lua .
Changes to any of them trigger a reload, since the whole directory is watched.
C modules cannot be loaded.
.PP
When the config is a symlink, both directories are searched: the one holding
the name and the one holding the target. A dotfiles repository keeps the
neighbouring modules beside the real file; a config manager publishes each of
them as its own symlink in
.IR ~/.config/clipmunge ,
so the siblings are beside the name instead. Searching both means the same
rule set works either way.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR clipmunge (1)
